CSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery) is a web security attack where a malicious website tricks a logged-in user
 into performing an unwanted action on another website where they are already authenticated.

Example

Suppose you're logged into your banking application.

The bank has an endpoint:

POST /transfer

with parameters:

{
  "toAccount": "12345",
  "amount": 1000
}

If the bank only relies on the session cookie for authentication, an attacker can create a malicious page:

<form action="https://bank.com/transfer" method="POST">
    <input type="hidden" name="toAccount" value="attacker123">
    <input type="hidden" name="amount" value="1000">
</form>

<script>
    document.forms[0].submit();
</script>

When the victim visits this page:

Browser automatically sends the bank's session cookie.
Request appears authenticated.
Money gets transferred without the user's consent.

This is a CSRF attack.

How CSRF is Prevented
1. CSRF Token (Most Common)

Server generates a random token and embeds it in forms.

<input type="hidden" name="_csrf" value="abc123xyz">

The server validates the token on form submission.

Without the correct token, the request is rejected.

2. SameSite Cookies

Set session cookie as:

Set-Cookie: JSESSIONID=123;
SameSite=Strict

or

SameSite=Lax

This prevents browsers from sending cookies in many cross-site requests.

3. Verify Origin/Referer Headers

Check:

Origin: https://yourwebsite.com

or

Referer: https://yourwebsite.com/profile

Reject requests coming from other domains.

CSRF vs XSS
| CSRF                               | XSS                                               |
| ---------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
| Tricks user into sending a request | Injects malicious JavaScript                      |
| Exploits trust of website in user  | Exploits trust of user in website                 |
| Requires authenticated user        | May not require authentication                    |
| Prevented using CSRF tokens        | Prevented by input validation and output encoding |

CSRF in Spring Security

Spring Security enables CSRF protection by default for web applications.

@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
public class SecurityConfig {

    @Bean
    SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        return http.build();
    }
}

To disable:

http.csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable());
Interview Question:

Why is CSRF often disabled in REST APIs?

Because REST APIs commonly use JWT tokens in the Authorization header instead of session cookies.
 Since browsers do not automatically attach JWTs to requests, CSRF attacks are generally not possible.


 If your application uses session-based authentication, then CSRF protection is required.

 Why?

 When a user logs in:

 Set-Cookie: JSESSIONID=ABC123

 The browser stores this cookie and automatically sends it with every request to your domain:

 Cookie: JSESSIONID=ABC123

 An attacker can exploit this behavior because the browser sends the session cookie even when a request originates from a malicious website.

 Flow of a CSRF Attack
    User logs into your application.
    Browser stores JSESSIONID.
    User visits a malicious website.
    Malicious website submits a request to your application.
    Browser automatically attaches JSESSIONID.
    Server thinks the request is from the authenticated user.

 How Spring Security Protects Against It

 When CSRF is enabled, Spring generates a token:

 <input type="hidden" name="_csrf" value="token123">

 The client must send this token with POST/PUT/DELETE requests.

 Example:

 POST /transfer
 Cookie: JSESSIONID=ABC123
 X-CSRF-TOKEN: token123

 The server validates the token before processing the request.

 Session vs JWT
| Feature                                 | Session-Based Auth | JWT Auth                  |
| --------------------------------------- | ------------------ | ------------------------- |
| Authentication stored in                | Server Session     | JWT Token                 |
| Browser sends credentials automatically | Yes (Cookie)       | No (Authorization Header) |
| Vulnerable to CSRF                      | Yes                | Usually No                |
| CSRF Token Needed                       | Yes                | Usually No                |

 Interview Answer

 Q: When should CSRF protection be enabled?

 CSRF protection should be enabled when authentication relies on cookies or server-side sessions because browsers automatically send cookies
 with requests. An attacker can exploit this behavior to perform unauthorized actions on behalf of the user.
 CSRF tokens help verify that the request originated from the legitimate application.

 Q: Why do many Spring Boot REST APIs disable CSRF?

 Because they use stateless authentication (JWT/Bearer tokens) in the Authorization header rather than session cookies.
 Since browsers do not automatically attach Authorization headers, CSRF attacks are generally not possible.